WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:13.960
Malcolm Grant: Let us move on then please
to the next item which is the paper on Genomics

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:18.770
and just recalling the declaration of interest
that I made earlier. I am pleased to invite

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:23.960
Tim to introduce the paper and it may be that
Bruce will wish to add comments in due course.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:30.960
Tim Kelsey: Just to pick up on that theme
of energy and commitment, as the paper describes

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:38.020
we have moved now to the point, and I will
update the Board shortly, on the eleven designated

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.739
Genomic Medicine Centres becoming operational.
I would like first of all to thank the extraordinary

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:50.280
efforts of individuals across England in those
eleven centres who have worked unbelievably

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:57.280
hard to get to the point where, quite literally,
the NHS is now leading the world and is at

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:05.690
the cutting edge of this very important science.
Alongside thanking the team in NHS England

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.090
who has done so much to support the GMCs and
then working closely with colleagues at Genomics

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.850
England to get us to this point.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:17.820
The point we are at now, just to update the
paper, is that, of course everyone will know

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:24.820
2012 the Prime Minister challenged the NHS
to be the first country in the world to sequence

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.079
100,000 genomes. Genomics England is working
to actually do those sequences and NHS England's

00:01:33.079 --> 00:01:38.979
responsibility is to support Genomics England
in achieving that objective. We have been

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:45.979
running a series of complex, very robust tender
processes to identify centres in the NHS that

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:53.409
will drive that process forward. The first
wave of selection concluded last year with

00:01:53.630 --> 00:02:00.380
the designation of eleven centres. In fact
the next wave of opportunity for local Trusts

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.420
to participate starts in April.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:09.420
Where we are now is that in fact, as of today,
we have six of the eleven Trusts that have

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.290
now signed up to the contract that we have
arranged for delivery of the 100,000 Genomes

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.650
Programme. Indeed one, Greater Manchester,
has already started to recruit participants.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:26.650
We are actually at the very start of this
very important programme. We expect the other

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.370
Genome Medical Centres to sign their contracts
and be in position to start equally recruiting

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:40.710
participants over the next three to four weeks.
We have gone from a point in 2012 to a point

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.410
today where we are now live with the programme.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:49.610
I think probably I was going to leave it there
unless people have of course questions about

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:53.540
the operation or delivery of the programme.
There are two outstanding questions which

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:59.090
perhaps I might have invite Bruce to speak
to. One is I think it is our view that we

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.480
need to start quite rapidly thinking about
what we, NHS England, Genomics England, the

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.800
Department, think is the next step for the
programme itself. The programme is meant to

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.600
conclude in 2017 so it is June we have to
offer some views to the incoming administration

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:20.300
about what shape of the Genomics Project itself
should be during the course of the next parliament.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.950
Equally there is a more fundamental question
to ask about what this implies for the shape

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:30.540
of clinical medicine over the next 10-20 years,
perhaps a programme we ought to be undertaking

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.440
today. If I could just hand over to Bruce?

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.510
Bruce Keogh: Tim, thanks very much. Chairman,
I would quite like to put Genomics in a slightly

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:46.510
wider context. It seems in a way that I would
hope to excite some ambition for the NHS.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:55.550
It seems to me that there is a confluence
of three sorts of forces. The first is, I

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.420
think, quite significant ambition for the
NHS, which is articulated in the NHS's own

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:08.420
vision, the Five Year Forward View and which
Ian has given some express examples of.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:17.180
The second is that there is a new science
globally which is advancing at pace. The first

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:22.400
of those is clearly genomics and we have discussed
that at this Board before. However, there

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.449
are two other areas where we are seeing a
lot of advance. One is in bionanotechnology

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:33.360
and the other is in material science. Then
the third force which is coming to play and

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:40.360
which in some ways links all of these is information
technology, in particular mobile digital technology.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:47.460
If I look at genomics, genomics will bring
in relatively short order the ability to both

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:54.770
predict individuals at risk of certain conditions
and populations at risk. It will give us the

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:01.550
opportunity to detect conditions much earlier
so, as you know, we may be able to shift from

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:07.479
biopsies for some types of cancers to blood
tests, which would be an enormous advantage,

00:05:07.479 --> 00:05:14.340
for example in detecting recurrence. It will
also give us the ability to give highly targeted

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:20.680
treatment for people with very specifically
defined conditions. So genomics has a huge

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.270
future for us.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:29.270
The second area is in the material science
and bionanotechnology. At the risk of sounding

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:39.180
like John's horoscope, is this new emerging
science of theranostics, which essentially

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:46.889
means the ability to diagnose something and
treat it with a degree of immediacy. An example

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:55.150
of that might be the ability for some implantable,
wearable or even swallowable device to detect,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:03.080
for example, a particular chemical in your
blood and dispense a drug to treat you according

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:11.699
to whatever it measures. That kind of technology
is not futuristic. It is already here. I think

00:06:13.879 --> 00:06:20.430
there is quite a big future in that which
will bring enormous benefits and relief to

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:21.169
many patients.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:28.169
Then, of course, we have IT and in particular
mobile IT which brings immediacy of knowledge,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:35.580
immediacy of information but also increasingly
immediacy of diagnostics to people and to

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:44.060
institutions and will give people huge personal
control over their own physiology and their

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.279
own health.

00:06:48.279 --> 00:06:55.279
I think there is a big opportunity for us
to try and explore what all of these converging

00:06:56.599 --> 00:07:02.229
forces mean for the NHS in the short- and
medium-term future. It would be difficult

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:09.229
to do it for the much longer term because
this is moving so fast. There are undoubtedly

00:07:10.159 --> 00:07:16.449
opportunities for us to reinforce the ambition
of the Five Year Forward View. What that means

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:23.189
both for individuals in terms of bringing
diagnostics not out of hospitals, into primary

00:07:23.189 --> 00:07:26.559
care and in many cases into people's own homes.

00:07:26.559 --> 00:07:33.559
I would like the Board to consider whether
we have a responsibility and a duty or an

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:39.969
opportunity to work with scientific and other
colleagues to try and understand what the

00:07:39.969 --> 00:07:46.759
confluence of these things mean for our NHS
and how we build a synergy that will promote

00:07:46.759 --> 00:07:48.860
the ambition that we have already laid out.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:55.860
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Tim and Bruce. Let
me open, please, the floor for discussion.

00:07:58.529 --> 00:08:05.529
John Burn: I probably have to make a declaration
of lifelong obsession, as a professional clinical

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:11.949
geneticist but also a declaration of interest
in the bionanotechnology as I chair a company

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:18.259
developing point-of-care diagnostic tools
in this field called QuantuMDx that I am obviously

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:21.710
fascinated by it and also deeply involved.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:26.960
What I would say is that Tim quite rightly
said we are at the cutting edge because what

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:33.960
we are trying to do here is deploy this new
technology in the context of an accredited

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:38.500
clinical service at scale. Lots of people
will claim we have sequenced everyone but

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:43.210
the question is would you put your life on
the data that they are producing? Can you

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:47.820
rely upon it and can you incorporate it into
healthcare? That is what the Genomic Medicine

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:52.640
Centres are going to have to try and cope
with. I think we need to be clear on that.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.130
It is not just a technology. It is much more
than that.

00:08:56.130 --> 00:09:03.130
The second thing to say is that the whole
genome application which we are both so involved

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:08.840
in has to be done at scale if it is to be
done at all, if you want to make it reliable

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:15.840
and replicable. That is why it does not need
a system shift to make it work. It is not

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:21.650
just about whole genomes. We can look at bits
of genomes and prevention and prediction.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:27.960
It is more than the 100,000 genomes. It is
sideways as well as in depth.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:34.960
What I would say is my seat here in this context
is to not be an obsessional train-spotter

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:40.850
but to actually separate myself from my own
enthusiasm and ask for the NHS, how do we

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:46.680
embed this in three years' time? It is very
easy for this to be one of those sky rockets

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.880
that goes up and then fizzles out and comes
back down again. That is a major challenge.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:57.360
The second major challenge is, to mix my metaphors,
not to allow the car to roll back on the hill

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:02.720
start. We are so busy trying to deploy this
new technology that the already existing resources

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.950
which are among the best in the world are
not properly looked after and we end up going

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.760
backwards while we are still busy trying to
go forwards. I think those are the two preoccupations

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:13.280
for me.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:18.670
I have no doubt that this is disruptive technology
and it is transformational, words that tend

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:24.140
to get overused. However, it is also dangerous
because of the very nature of its transformational

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:31.030
disruption. It could damage what we have and
not achieve what it could promise to do. I

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:36.590
think there is a major burden on us to make
sure we do both of those things.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:42.590
Malcolm Grant: Thank you. Let me bring in
Ciaran and Moira.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:47.140
Ciaran Devane: This is not meant to be pouring
on cold water but there is one more dimension

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:52.520
which is we need to think impact in all of
this. It is not only here is the disruptive

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:57.910
stuff and let us make sure we use the assets
that exist at the clever end but also we need

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:03.060
to take the portfolio view and say, 'Actually
maybe what we really need is 20 physiotherapists,

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:10.060
not this latest piece of multi-million pound
kit as well.' Somewhere in all of this, if

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:17.200
you think through the whole spectrum is can
we not forget some of the really basic stuff

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.830
as well and say, 'Actually what we really
need is one of Ian's clever care models which

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:26.700
involve physios and the other stuff can arrive
in three years' time. Or vice versa.' Have

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:31.050
the whole thing in our heads, not just the
new bits.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.500
Speaker: Yes. I could not agree more.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:36.540
Speaker: I totally agree.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.650
Malcolm Grant: Right, that is a rare outbreak
of consensus. Noel and Moira.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:46.650
Noel Gordon: I wanted to respond, Malcolm,
to Bruce's question around whether the NHS

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.830
should do a piece of work specifically to
look at the confluence of the three forces

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:57.830
you mentioned. You will know that the Minister
for Life Sciences has launched, as part of

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:04.820
a government initiative, a Review of Innovative
Medicines and Related Technologies, which

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:11.350
John and myself sit on the Advisory Group
for that committee. Having seen what they

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:18.350
plan to do, which is really to accelerate
practical innovations that will make a difference

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:25.190
to many areas of specialised commissioning
in particular, I feel increasingly that we,

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:31.110
the NHS, need a point of view to inform what
that committee is doing. I do not know if

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:36.350
John agrees, but having attended the first
kick-off meeting only last week, I think this

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:42.610
is a bit of a gap, as you defined it, where
the expression of a point of view from our

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:49.610
side would massively inform the direction
in which that important review is going to

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:54.120
go. I would support what you suggest.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:55.360
Bruce Keogh: Yes.

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.260
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. Moira?

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:05.260
Moira Gibbs: Can I just ask where the responsibility
lies for getting the balance right between

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:12.320
protection of intellectual property and commercial
opportunities as it were? Is that with NHS

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:16.500
England or somewhere else in the complicated
system?

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:23.500
Tim Kelsey: The government structure is complex
but it is working and it is relatively straightforward

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:31.000
which is that NHS and Genomics England both
have their own boards. The programme itself

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:35.130
is accountable through the Department of Health
so the SRO for this programme, the 100,000

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:39.060
programme, is in the Department of Health
and reports that way up through the normal

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:44.840
mechanisms. In terms of judging some of these
very important issues, one of which you have

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:48.160
described. There are other ethical issues.
There are big issues around consent which

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:54.610
we need to get right. Essentially, it is a
collaborative endeavour which finally reaches

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:57.380
a point, through the Department of Health.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:02.570
Proposals have been made around things like
trying to manage the relationship between

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:09.570
potential economic opportunity that is going
to be developed, 'if it works', against the

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:16.600
human right that there should be no costs
or charge for people to take clinical benefit

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:22.980
from the output. I think there is a process
for providing proper governance, accountability,

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:27.970
around resolving those issues.
If I may, just to pick up on some of the points,

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:33.660
I think if I could also in a way agree with
the proposal that we do do some work on this.

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:40.660
I think we have to answer Ciaran's question,
which is that this is so untested in the real

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:46.940
world environment of a National Health Service
at scale. We do not know how long it will

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:52.440
take to produce the clinical value from the
linkage of phenotypic and routine and genomic

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:57.490
data etc. etc. It may take longer than we
expect, it costs us more than we expect.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:01.620
However, what we need to do is to start doing
some, I think, rather detailed economic modelling

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:08.570
to support the case for prioritising this
against a physiotherapist in a vanguard, as

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.510
a concept. I think that is something that
will inform George Freeman's Review in a very

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:19.100
important, practical way because there is
always a risk that the horizon-scanning excitement

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:25.540
does potentially distract attention from the
real world needs of the NHS.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:30.840
Speaker: [Inaudible] specialised commissioning
decisions which need forward view of what

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.280
is coming over the next three years.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:38.340
Victor Adebowale: It was just a minor point
but I think just to add to that I am increasingly

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:48.720
interested in the sociology of all this. It
is not just the technology, the business case.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:56.660
It is also something about who are we seeking
to affect? How do we make those choices? We

00:15:57.040 --> 00:16:03.400
had the conversation earlier, which diseases,
which bits of the community and how transparent

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:09.600
is that? I do not know who is going to do
this but it seems to me that we need a safe

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:15.980
view of the sociological impact alongside
the point that Ciaran very usefully made.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.030
It feels a bit haphazard.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:25.030
John Burn: Could I come back on that, Victor?
That is a very big question. I think, just

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:32.880
as a reassurance, is that I think ten of the
eleven Genomic Medicine Centres have at their

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:38.420
core, as the actual leadership, the regional
genetic centre or member of the Regional Genetic

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:42.600
Centre. The only one that is not, I know is
a surgeon, but he is a close friend and worked

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:48.720
in genetics for a long time. The point is,
as an organisation, the NHS has been a world

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:55.720
leader in medical and clinical genetics. Genomics
is just an expansion of that. Out of the inherited

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:03.120
traits, because we invented the word genetics
100 years ago, into genomics, which is about

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:08.909
looking at your whole printout. Actually that
word was invented by my old boss for a name

00:17:08.909 --> 00:17:14.959
for a new journal in 1975. We are basically
shifting from just looking at hereditary traits

00:17:14.959 --> 00:17:18.159
into looking at people's total printout.

00:17:18.159 --> 00:17:24.259
It is a major step but we have already taken
the first step by embedding genetics into

00:17:24.259 --> 00:17:29.340
our healthcare system and having a very well-established
Regional Genetics Network, which works closely

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:35.610
together. I think that is a great safety net
for making sure we deploy this. Many of the

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:40.169
ideas and concepts have been thought through
before we had the technology. Whereas in many

00:17:40.169 --> 00:17:44.480
other settings there is no genetics service,
therefore they are just dropping it in from

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.639
a great height. It will cause major problems.

00:17:46.639 --> 00:17:52.749
I think that if we do not ignore the expertise
and knowledge of the Regional Genetics Centres

00:17:52.749 --> 00:17:56.840
in its deployment, we will be well-placed,
because I think down the line you can see

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:02.649
that what we will be expecting is that multi-disciplinary
teams including those geneticists will think

00:18:02.649 --> 00:18:09.649
on the ground how best to do deploy this technology.
That will inevitably take account of many

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:17.320
of the sociological and economic factors that
you are preoccupied by. Deciding how you spend

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:22.919
this money, as Ciaran is really alluding to,
is critically important. You can easily do

00:18:22.919 --> 00:18:26.490
a lot of fancy work but not actually save
many lives.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:31.919
Malcolm Grant: I think that has been a very
valuable discussion. I think we should not

00:18:31.919 --> 00:18:37.450
go away from this assuming that this is straightforward
and easy. There are many complexities. The

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:43.480
first is the complexity of the relationship
between us and Genomics England Ltd. That

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:50.480
has involved a lot of hard work and partnership
to make that work because the long-term objectives

00:18:50.499 --> 00:18:57.139
are identical. The short term into actions
have been quite difficult from time to time.

00:18:57.139 --> 00:19:02.580
I think, secondly, we started off with an
assumption that the really difficult stuff

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:08.539
was the sequencing. It is not. The cost of
sequencing has plummeted. The efficiency,

00:19:08.539 --> 00:19:13.470
the high throughput and the accuracy have
continued to rise. It is Moore's Law with

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:18.039
a factor of multiples in terms of the transformation
of the technology.

00:19:18.039 --> 00:19:22.679
By the way, the sequencing will be done in
this country. I think it is a very nice coincidence

00:19:22.679 --> 00:19:29.679
that following the discovery of DNA in Cambridge,
following the sequencing of the first human

00:19:31.490 --> 00:19:37.440
genome in Cambridge, plus the US, the sequencing
for this will be done in Cambridge at a new

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.679
facility funded by the Wellcome.

00:19:39.679 --> 00:19:46.679
I think, though, that one of the major complexities
is getting the samples, particularly in cancer,

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:56.940
to a level of accuracy and with sufficient
DNA in them to make them successful for the

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:04.710
sequencing. The second and much more complex
is actually the interpretation. We are not

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:11.710
there. There is a long way to go yet in terms
of understanding the printouts and their correlation

00:20:13.749 --> 00:20:18.440
with particular types of disease. We are probably
more successful than the early stages with

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:24.360
rare diseases and with cancer, which is why
those two have been chosen as the two leading

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:25.169
operations.

00:20:25.169 --> 00:20:30.690
Two aspects to this. First of all, Genomics
England has set up GCIP, the Genomics Clinical

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:34.960
Interpretation Partnership, which is involving
clinicians from across the country, coming

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:41.960
together in groups and focusing on particular
areas of disease. Secondly, trying to create

00:20:44.809 --> 00:20:51.809
a structure in which we can bring in expertise
from around the world to help us solve some

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:57.610
of the big problems with the data and trying
to make that flexible. We are using Illumina

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:02.919
probably to some extent who are our sequencers,
but using other organisations just to help

00:21:02.919 --> 00:21:07.269
us to understand what the data means. That
is the foundation stone for the vision that

00:21:07.269 --> 00:21:13.690
Bruce was outlining as to how that then starts
to help us in prediction and in therapeutics.

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:20.690
It is a long way to go and even after the
first 100,000 has been sequenced we will continue

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:28.499
to learn from those results for years and
years to follow. What we would like to bring

00:21:28.499 --> 00:21:33.409
back to the Board is, I think, what is the
implication for the NHS? We never wanted this

00:21:33.409 --> 00:21:38.860
to be a research project. It is not a research
project. It is a clinical transformation project

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:45.039
and I think we would therefore very much want
to pick up on your offer, Bruce and Tim, to

00:21:45.039 --> 00:21:48.309
come back to the Board with a paper that explores
the future implications.

00:21:48.309 --> 00:21:54.700
Bruce Keogh: Chairman, may I just be clear
that I think it is important this covers more

00:21:54.700 --> 00:22:01.700
than just genomics, so that genomics is, if
you like, the fulcrum for the discussion.

00:22:01.909 --> 00:22:03.830
The other forces are really very important.

00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:04.210
Malcolm Grant: Yes, indeed.

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:10.690
Bruce Keogh: We do not even know the magnitude
of the problem so I think Victor's comments

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:17.690
about we could end up in some kind of haphazard
place is a risk if we do not at least explore

00:22:18.639 --> 00:22:19.240
the terrain.

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:24.340
Malcolm Grant: Yes. Molecular pathology. Right.
Thank you very much Bruce and Tim.

